WB.L("ring-of-gyges", "philosophy", "beginner", "The ring of Gyges: would you be good if no one could see?",
  "A shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible, and within days he has seduced a queen and murdered a king. Plato used the story to ask the most uncomfortable question in ethics.",
  [
    ["A ring in a chasm", "In Plato's Republic, a shepherd finds a ring of invisibility and uses it to seize a kingdom.",
      "Early in Plato's Republic, written around 375 BC, a young man named Glaucon tells a story. A shepherd called Gyges is tending his flock in Lydia, in what is now western Turkey, when an earthquake splits the ground. In the chasm he finds a hollow bronze horse holding the body of a giant, wearing only a gold ring. Gyges takes it.\n\nLater he discovers that turning the ring's setting towards his palm makes him invisible, and turning it back makes him visible again. He wastes no time. He gets himself sent to the palace as a messenger, seduces the queen, kills the king with her help and takes the throne."],
    ["Glaucon's challenge", "Glaucon argues that people are just only because they fear being caught, and asks whether justice is worth having once every reward is removed.",
      "Glaucon, one of Plato's older brothers, imagines two such rings: one given to a just man, one to an unjust man. He bets that both would behave the same way, taking whatever they pleased. Nobody is so iron-willed, he says, as to resist when there is no risk. If so, justice is not valued for itself. It is a compromise: we avoid harming each other because we fear being harmed.\n\nHe then raises the stakes. A perfectly unjust man with a spotless reputation grows rich and admired, while a perfectly just man branded wicked is whipped, imprisoned and executed. Which life is better? Glaucon and his brother Adeimantus ask Socrates to prove that justice benefits the just person even when every outside reward is stripped away."],
    ["Socrates' long answer", "Plato's reply is that justice is the health of the soul, with reason ruling over spirit and appetite.",
      "Answering the challenge takes up most of the rest of the Republic. Socrates argues that the soul has three parts: reason, which seeks truth; spirit, which feels pride, anger and honour; and appetite, which craves food, sex and money.\n\nIn a just person, reason rules, spirit supports it and appetite is kept in its place. In an unjust person, the parts are at war, and appetite or ambition takes control. The unjust ruler with the magic ring is therefore not free at all. He is a slave to cravings that can never be satisfied: restless, suspicious and alone. Justice, Plato concludes, is to the soul what health is to the body, and no amount of wealth makes up for a sick soul."],
    ["Another Gyges", "Herodotus tells an older, ring-free version in which the queen forces Gyges to choose between killing the king and dying.",
      "Gyges may have been a real person. A king of Lydia by that name ruled in the 7th century BC and founded a dynasty that later included the famously wealthy Croesus.\n\nThe historian Herodotus, writing in the 5th century BC, tells a very different tale of his rise. King Candaules is so proud of his wife's beauty that he insists his bodyguard Gyges hide in the royal bedroom to see her undressed. The queen spots him and gives him a stark choice: kill the king and marry her, or die. He chooses the throne. There is no magic ring in Herodotus, but both stories share hidden watching and power gained by stealth."],
    ["The test of the ring", "The ring tests whether you would act the same way when no one is watching.",
      "Readers often compare Gyges' ring to the One Ring in Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings, which also grants invisibility and corrupts its wearer. Whether Tolkien had Plato in mind is uncertain. Today many people carry a weaker version in their pockets: anonymous accounts, deleted messages and private browsing offer a taste of acting unseen.\n\nThe thought experiment lasts because it is so personal. It asks not what rules a society needs, but what you would do if consequences vanished. Try a small ring: a mistake in your favour at the till, an unwatched moment at work. Some philosophers say Plato's answer merely redefines justice as inner balance. Even so, the old saying holds: integrity is doing the right thing when no one is watching."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does Gyges use the ring once he discovers its power?", ["He hands it to the king as a gift","He returns it to the chasm and forgets it","He gets into the palace, seduces the queen and seizes the throne","He uses it to guard his flock at night"], 2, "Gyges uses invisibility for personal gain, showing what Glaucon thinks anyone would do without fear of being caught."],
    [1, "What is Glaucon's main claim about justice?", ["People are just mainly because they fear being caught","Justice is a gift from the gods","Only rulers can be truly just","Justice is impossible to define"], 0, "Glaucon argues justice is a compromise born of fear: give anyone a ring of invisibility and they would behave unjustly."],
    [2, "According to Socrates in the Republic, what is justice in a person?", ["Following the law as exactly as possible","Keeping a good reputation in the city","Reason ruling spirit and appetite, like health of the soul","Giving away your wealth to the poor"], 2, "Plato describes justice as the right order of the soul's three parts, comparable to health in the body."],
    [3, "How does Herodotus' version of Gyges differ from Plato's?", ["Gyges refuses the throne in the end","The story is set in Egypt, not Lydia","Gyges is a god rather than a man","There is no ring; the queen forces Gyges to choose"], 3, "In Herodotus, the queen gives Gyges a choice of killing the king or dying, and no magic is involved."],
    [4, "What does the ring of Gyges ask you to consider?", ["Whether invisibility will ever be possible","Whether you act the same when no one is watching","Whether kings should be elected by citizens","Whether ancient myths are literally true"], 1, "The thought experiment asks what you would do if consequences vanished, which shows whether your goodness is genuinely your own."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A shepherd finds a ring and becomes king within days. Would you do the same?"});

WB.L("diogenes-the-cynic", "philosophy", "beginner", "Diogenes: the philosopher who lived in a jar",
  "He slept in a storage jar, carried a lamp in daylight and reportedly told Alexander the Great to get out of his sunlight. Behind the stunts was a serious idea about what we really need.",
  [
    ["Deface the currency", "Diogenes turned an exile over defaced coins into a motto: challenge the false values society stamps onto things.",
      "Diogenes was born around 412 BC in Sinope, a Greek trading city on the Black Sea. Almost everything we know comes from stories gathered centuries later, especially by the biographer Diogenes Laertius, so many details are legend.\n\nBy these accounts, Diogenes or his father, who was in charge of the city's money, was caught defacing the coinage, and Diogenes was exiled. He turned the incident into a motto: deface the currency. He meant challenging the false values society stamps onto things, such as wealth, status, fashion and good manners. Arriving in Athens, he set about testing which values were genuine and which were counterfeit."],
    ["Life in a jar", "Diogenes lived with almost nothing to show how little a person needs, and the insult 'dog' gave the Cynics their name.",
      "In Athens, Diogenes lived in a large ceramic storage jar near the marketplace. He owned a cloak, a staff and a bag, and little else. One story says he had a drinking cup until he saw a boy drinking from his cupped hands. He threw the cup away, declaring that a child had beaten him in simplicity.\n\nHe ate, slept and did everything else in public, scandalising respectable citizens. They called him kyon, meaning dog. He embraced the insult, and his followers became known as Cynics, from the Greek for doglike. Their aim was to live according to nature, free of needless wants."],
    ["Lamp and sunlight", "Diogenes' stunts were pointed lessons, and his reply to Alexander shows that a person who wants nothing cannot be bought.",
      "Diogenes was a performer as much as a thinker. He reportedly walked the streets in daylight with a lit lamp, saying he was looking for a human being, meaning an honest one. When Plato defined a human as a featherless creature that walks on two legs, Diogenes supposedly plucked a chicken and announced, 'Here is Plato's human.'\n\nIn the most famous tale, Alexander the Great found him lying in the sun in Corinth and asked if he could do anything for him. 'Yes,' said Diogenes, 'stand out of my sunlight.' Alexander reportedly said that if he were not Alexander, he would wish to be Diogenes. Historians cannot confirm the meeting. Its point still strikes: someone who wants nothing cannot be bribed, flattered or threatened."],
    ["Citizen of the world", "Diogenes called himself a citizen of the world, an idea the Stoics later developed.",
      "Asked where he came from, Diogenes reportedly said he was a kosmopolites, a citizen of the world, the origin of our word cosmopolitan. In ancient Greece this was radical: identity was bound to one's city, and exile was among the worst punishments. If what matters is living well according to nature, Diogenes implied, the borders and customs of any one city are secondary.\n\nThe idea travelled. Crates of Thebes, his most important follower, gave away a fortune to live as a Cynic. He married Hipparchia, a wealthy woman who chose that life and became one of the few women philosophers of ancient Greece whose name survives. Crates taught Zeno, founder of the Stoics, who developed the idea into all humans belonging to one community of reason."],
    ["What he still teaches", "Diogenes asks which of our wants are real needs and which are only what society tells us to want.",
      "Diogenes died in Corinth, traditionally in 323 BC, the same year as Alexander, though the dates are uncertain. The Cynics were street philosophers with no building or fixed curriculum. Their legacy was two ideas: virtue is enough for happiness, and most social conventions are optional.\n\nFew people would want to live as he did, but his challenge holds up. How many of our wants are real needs, and how many are what society tells us to want? Modern minimalism and the urge to declutter echo him. A simple test: of any possession or ambition, ask whether losing it would leave you truly worse off, or only less admired."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Diogenes mean by his motto 'deface the currency'?", ["He wanted to abolish money entirely","He wanted to design better coins","He wanted to challenge society's false values","He was confessing to a crime in public"], 2, "The motto came from a coin scandal, but Diogenes used it to expose counterfeit values such as status and wealth."],
    [1, "Why did Diogenes live with almost nothing?", ["He had lost his fortune in exile","To show how little a person really needs","He had taken a religious vow of silence","The Athenians had banned him from owning property"], 1, "He deliberately lived simply, free of needless wants, to prove that a person needs very little."],
    [2, "What does Diogenes' reply to Alexander, 'stand out of my sunlight', illustrate?", ["Someone who wants nothing cannot be bought or threatened","Kings should be honoured above all other people","Sunlight is a gift that comes from the gods","Philosophers should serve their rulers loyally"], 0, "Because Diogenes wanted nothing from Alexander, even the most powerful man in the world had nothing to offer him."],
    [3, "What did Diogenes imply by calling himself a 'citizen of the world'?", ["He had visited every city in Greece","Living well matters more than one city's borders","He rejected all friendship and family","Only Athenians were truly civilised people"], 1, "If the aim is living well according to nature, the laws and borders of any one city are secondary."],
    [4, "Which question reflects the test inspired by Diogenes?", ["How can I make this possession last for many years?","How does my version of this compare with what others own?","What will this ambition or possession be worth in ten years?","Would losing this really leave me worse off, or only less admired?"], 3, "The test separates real needs from wants that society has taught us to chase."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"He lived in a jar and told the world's most powerful man to move. Why?"});

WB.L("drowning-child", "philosophy", "beginner", "The drowning child: Peter Singer’s challenge",
  "You would ruin your shoes to save a child drowning in front of you. An Australian philosopher asked why distance should make any difference, and the question changed how millions think about giving.",
  [
    ["The shallow pond", "Singer's principle: if we can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to.",
      "Imagine you are walking past a shallow pond and see a child drowning. You could wade in, but you would ruin your clothes. Almost everyone agrees you must save the child. Peter Singer, a philosopher born in Melbourne in 1946, used this scene in his 1972 essay Famine, Affluence, and Morality. The cost there was muddy clothes; later retellings added expensive shoes.\n\nHe wrote it during the 1971 crisis in East Pakistan, now Bangladesh, where war, a devastating cyclone and mass displacement left millions of refugees facing hunger and disease. Aid from rich nations fell far short. His principle: if we can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it."],
    ["Distance and bystanders", "Singer argues that neither distance nor the inaction of others reduces our duty to help.",
      "The argument's power lies in the next step. If you must ruin your clothes to save the child in the pond, why not spend the same amount to save a child dying of a preventable disease far away? Singer argued that distance in itself has no moral weight. A child's life is not worth less because it is further from you. Nearness makes suffering more vivid, not more important.\n\nAnother objection is that millions of people could help, so the burden cannot fall on me. Singer imagined several adults standing around the pond, all ignoring the child. That does not make it acceptable for you to walk past too. Others' failure does not cancel your duty, so giving is an obligation, not just generosity."],
    ["How much is enough?", "Taken strictly, Singer's principle asks a great deal, so he offered weaker versions and realistic guidelines.",
      "Followed to its conclusion, Singer's principle is extremely demanding. If you should give whenever it costs you nothing of comparable importance, you should keep giving until giving more would harm you or your dependants as much as it helps others.\n\nSinger accepted this but also offered a weaker version: we should at least prevent bad outcomes when the cost to us is genuinely insignificant, such as a new jacket we do not need. In later books, including The Life You Can Save in 2009, he suggested more modest guidelines based on income. A realistic standard that most people actually meet, he argued, does more good than a perfect one they ignore."],
    ["Effective altruism", "Effective altruism asks us to use evidence to find where giving does the most good.",
      "Singer's ideas helped shape the effective altruism movement, which emerged in the late 2000s. Its central claim is that if we give, we should use evidence to find where our money or time does the most good.\n\nIn 2009, the Oxford philosophers Toby Ord and William MacAskill helped found Giving What We Can, whose members pledge to give at least 10 per cent of their income to highly effective charities. Charity evaluators compare causes such as malaria prevention and deworming by estimating their cost per life improved. The movement has drawn both admiration and controversy, especially after the 2022 collapse of the crypto exchange FTX, whose founder had been a prominent supporter."],
    ["The critics answer back", "Many philosophers accept part of Singer's argument but reject its most demanding form.",
      "Critics have pushed back in several ways. Following the English philosopher Bernard Williams, some argue that a morality demanding constant sacrifice leaves no room for the personal projects, friendships and commitments that give life meaning.\n\nOthers say the pond is too simple: a single rescue is certain and immediate, while aid to distant countries is uncertain, can be misdirected, and does not tackle the political causes of poverty. Still others defend special duties to family and community. Yet even many critics concede Singer's basic point that wealthy people can do a great deal of good at little cost to themselves. More than fifty years on, the shallow pond remains a test many readers find hard to walk past."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the core of Singer's principle?", ["Charity is admirable but entirely optional","We ought to prevent great harm when the cost to us is minor","Only governments have any duty to help others","We should give away absolutely everything we own"], 1, "The pond case shows we accept this principle when the child is near, so Singer asks why it should change with distance."],
    [1, "What is Singer's view of physical distance?", ["Distance makes our duty stronger","Distance in itself has no moral weight","We owe nothing to people outside our country","Distance matters for adults but not for children"], 1, "A child's life is not worth less because the child is far away, even though nearby suffering feels more vivid."],
    [2, "Why did Singer suggest modest guidelines based on income?", ["He thought charity should be avoided altogether","He wanted only the very richest people to give","People follow a realistic standard more than a perfect one","He decided the pond example was misleading"], 2, "Singer accepted that his strict principle is very demanding, so he proposed guidelines most people could realistically follow."],
    [3, "What is the core idea of effective altruism?", ["Giving only to local causes","Giving only through religious groups","Giving nothing until you are wealthy","Using evidence to find where giving does the most good"], 3, "Effective altruism asks people to compare causes carefully so their money or time helps as many people as possible."],
    [4, "What is one criticism of Singer's demanding view of giving?", ["Constant sacrifice leaves no room for personal commitments","Wealthy people can do very little good by giving","Distant suffering is not real suffering at all","The pond example is far too complicated to follow"], 0, "Critics such as Bernard Williams argue a morality that demands constant sacrifice crowds out friendships and personal projects."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"You would ruin your shoes to save a child. An Australian asked why distance matters."});

WB.L("experience-machine", "philosophy", "beginner", "The experience machine: would you plug in?",
  "Imagine a machine that could give you any life you wanted, every moment indistinguishable from reality. Most people say they would refuse, and that refusal tells us something surprising about happiness.",
  [
    ["A perfect simulation", "Robert Nozick imagined a machine that could give you any experiences you wanted, indistinguishable from real life.",
      "In 1974, the American philosopher Robert Nozick described a thought experiment in his book Anarchy, State, and Utopia. Suppose brilliant neuroscientists could build a machine that stimulates your brain so you would think and feel you were writing a great novel, making a friend or reading an interesting book. All the while you would actually be floating in a tank with electrodes attached to your head.\n\nYou could choose your experiences from a vast library, and you would not know you were plugged in. Every moment would feel completely real. Nozick even allowed for occasional breaks to choose the next stretch. Then he asked: should you plug in for life?"],
    ["Why most say no", "Most people refuse the machine, which suggests we value more than pleasant experience and challenges hedonism.",
      "Many people, when first asked, say they would not plug in, even though the machine promises delight, achievement and love. That reaction matters because of Nozick's target: hedonism, the view that pleasant experience is the only thing good in itself. The 18th-century English philosopher Jeremy Bentham built his utilitarianism on the idea that pleasure and pain are all that ultimately matter.\n\nIf hedonism were true, the machine would be an easy choice, since it can deliver better feelings than real life ever could. Hesitation suggests we value something beyond our own experiences. If you feel a real but imperfect life beats a perfect simulated one, you are not a pure hedonist. It became one of the most discussed thought experiments of the 20th century."],
    ["Doing, being and reality", "Nozick gave three reasons the machine falls short: we want to do things, to be a certain kind of person, and to touch reality.",
      "Nozick suggested three things the machine cannot give us. First, we want to actually do certain things, not just have the experience of doing them. We want to really write the novel, not merely feel as if we had.\n\nSecond, we want to be a certain kind of person. In the tank you would be, as Nozick put it, an indeterminate blob, with no genuine courage, kindness or wit, because you never act. Third, the machine limits us to a man-made reality, a world no deeper than its programmers imagined. Plugging in, he wrote, is a kind of suicide. Together these points suggest a good life involves real contact with the world and real achievement, not just good feelings."],
    ["The reversal", "Later philosophers asked whether our refusal reflects deep truth or only a bias towards the familiar.",
      "Not everyone accepts Nozick's conclusion. Some philosophers argue that our reluctance to plug in might reflect a bias towards the familiar, sometimes called status quo bias, rather than a deep truth about value.\n\nTo test this, the philosopher Felipe De Brigard reversed the scenario in a 2010 study. He asked people to imagine discovering that they were already in an experience machine, and that their real life outside was very different. Would they unplug? Many said they would rather stay. If people prefer to keep the life they know, real or simulated, then the original intuition may be less about reality and more about fear of losing what is familiar. The debate continues."],
    ["What the machine reveals", "The machine separates feeling good from doing good, which helps you see what you believe makes a life go well.",
      "When The Matrix appeared in 1999, many viewers recognised Nozick's idea. The character Cypher bites into a steak he knows is not real and decides he would rather return to the comfortable illusion. Today virtual reality, immersive games and feeds tuned to our preferences offer partial versions of the machine. None comes close, but each asks how much of your time you want to spend in experiences rather than reality.\n\nThe machine is useful because it separates what ordinary life bundles together: feeling good and doing good. Some philosophers say well-being includes real relationships, knowledge and authenticity; others defend hedonism. Either way, ask yourself: would you plug in? If not, what would you miss? Your answer summarises what you think life is for."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does Nozick's experience machine do?", ["Records your memories for later viewing","Lets you travel through time","Makes any experience you choose feel completely real","Reads other people's thoughts"], 2, "The machine creates experiences indistinguishable from reality while you float in a tank, unaware you are plugged in."],
    [1, "What does widespread refusal to plug in suggest?", ["We value things beyond pleasant experience","Pleasure is the only thing that matters","People fear technology","Real life is always better than any simulation"], 0, "If pleasant experience were all that mattered, plugging in would be an easy choice, so hesitation hints at other values."],
    [2, "Which is one of Nozick's reasons for refusing the machine?", ["The machine might break down someday","We would miss our food and our sleep","We want to really do things, not merely feel we did","It would cost far too much money"], 2, "Nozick argued we want to act, to be a certain kind of person and to be in contact with a reality deeper than a programmed one."],
    [3, "What did Felipe De Brigard's reversed scenario suggest?", ["Everyone always prefers reality","Refusal may partly reflect liking the familiar","Nobody would ever use such a machine","Pleasure is completely meaningless"], 1, "Many people told they were already in a machine chose to stay, hinting that status quo bias may shape the original intuition."],
    [4, "Why is the experience machine a useful thought experiment?", ["It proves that hedonism is correct","It predicts which technology will succeed","It separates feeling good from doing good","It shows that virtual reality is harmful"], 2, "By pulling apart feeling good and doing good, the machine forces you to say what you think makes a life go well."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A machine offers a perfect life you could never tell was fake. Most refuse. Why?"});

WB.L("zeno-paradoxes", "philosophy", "beginner", "Zeno’s paradoxes: why Achilles can never win",
  "Around 2,500 years ago, a Greek thinker ‘proved’ that a runner can never catch a tortoise and that a flying arrow never moves. It took mathematicians two thousand years to say clearly what was wrong.",
  [
    ["A defender of the One", "Zeno devised his paradoxes to defend his teacher's claim that change and motion are illusions.",
      "Zeno lived in Elea, a Greek colony in southern Italy, in the 5th century BC. He followed the philosopher Parmenides, who argued that reality is a single, unchanging whole and that motion and change are illusions of the senses. Most people found this absurd.\n\nZeno's response was clever. Rather than prove Parmenides directly, he showed that the ordinary belief in motion leads to absurdities of its own. He reportedly wrote a book of around forty such arguments, but it has not survived. We know his most famous paradoxes mainly through Aristotle, who described them in his Physics in order to refute them. Plato's dialogue Parmenides depicts Zeno visiting Athens with his teacher."],
    ["Racing a tortoise", "Zeno argued that dividing a journey into endless stages makes catching a tortoise, or even starting to walk, seem impossible.",
      "Achilles, the swiftest hero of Greek legend, races a tortoise given a head start. When Achilles reaches where the tortoise began, it has crept ahead a little. When he reaches that point, it has moved again, by less. This continues forever, so Zeno concluded Achilles can never catch it. Real runners overtake slower ones all the time. The challenge is to explain exactly where the reasoning goes wrong.\n\nThe dichotomy is stranger still. To reach a door, you must first walk halfway, before that a quarter, before that an eighth, without end. With no first step, it seems you can never start. A later story says Diogenes answered by simply walking across the room, which shows motion happens but not why the argument fails."],
    ["The arrow that never flies", "Zeno argued that an arrow is at rest at every instant, so it seems it can never move.",
      "The arrow paradox works differently. Picture an arrow in flight. At any single instant, it occupies a space exactly equal to its own length. In that instant it is not moving, because an instant has no duration in which movement could happen.\n\nBut the whole flight is made up of instants. If the arrow is at rest in every one of them, when does it actually move? Zeno concluded that motion is impossible. The paradox puzzled thinkers for centuries because it seems to attack the very idea of something moving at a particular moment. Aristotle replied that time is not made of indivisible instants, just as a line is not made of points laid end to end."],
    ["Infinite sums that add up", "Infinitely many shrinking steps can add up to a finite distance, covered in a finite time.",
      "Much of the modern answer lies in mathematics. Add a half, a quarter, an eighth, a sixteenth and so on forever, and the total never exceeds one. It gets closer and closer to one, and mathematicians say the infinite sum equals exactly one.\n\nSo infinitely many steps can add up to a finite distance. If each step takes proportionally less time, they can be completed in a finite time, and Achilles catches the tortoise at a precise, calculable point. Ancient mathematicians glimpsed this idea, but the rigorous theory of limits that makes it precise was developed in the 19th century by mathematicians including Augustin-Louis Cauchy and Karl Weierstrass. Calculus also defines speed at an instant as a limit."],
    ["Still puzzling, still useful", "Zeno shows that when a valid-looking argument reaches an absurd conclusion, a hidden assumption deserves a closer look.",
      "Most mathematicians regard the paradoxes as resolved. Some philosophers are less sure. The mathematics shows the infinite sum has a finite value, but critics ask how anyone can complete an infinite sequence of distinct tasks one after another. Thought experiments about supertasks ask whether a lamp switched on and off infinitely many times in two minutes is on or off at the end. Physics adds a twist: it is an open question whether space and time are infinitely divisible.\n\nZeno did not change anyone's mind about whether things move. He showed that our ideas about space, time and infinity were less clear than we thought. Bertrand Russell praised his arguments as immeasurably subtle and profound."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Zeno create his paradoxes?", ["To prove the gods control motion","To win a famous running race","To defend his teacher's claim that motion is an illusion","To invent a new kind of mathematics"], 2, "Zeno aimed to show that belief in motion leads to absurdities, supporting his teacher's claim that reality is unchanging."],
    [1, "In the Achilles paradox, why can Achilles supposedly never catch the tortoise?", ["Each time he reaches where it was, it has moved ahead","The tortoise is secretly faster than he is","Achilles tires before he reaches the finish","The track is infinitely long in total"], 0, "Zeno splits the race into endless catching-up stages, each leaving the tortoise slightly ahead."],
    [2, "What is the arrow paradox's central claim?", ["Arrows always miss their intended target","Arrows travel faster than the speed of sound","At each instant the arrow is at rest, so it seems not to move","An arrow can be in two places at once"], 2, "An instant has no duration for movement, so an arrow made of resting instants seems unable to move at all."],
    [3, "What does the sum of a half, a quarter, an eighth and so on forever equal?", ["Infinity","One half","Zero","One"], 3, "The series converges to exactly one, so infinitely many shrinking steps can add up to a finite distance."],
    [4, "What lesson does Zeno offer for good thinking?", ["Absurd conclusions should simply be accepted as true","Arguments that seem valid should always be ignored","Reasoning about infinity should never be trusted","If an argument seems valid but absurd, look for its flawed assumption"], 3, "Finding the flaw in Zeno's reasoning took centuries and taught us a great deal about infinity and limits."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A runner can never catch a tortoise, or so a Greek thinker 'proved'. Where's the flaw?"});

WB.L("pascals-wager", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Pascal’s wager: betting on God",
  "A brilliant 17th century mathematician argued that believing in God is simply the smart bet. His reasoning helped invent decision theory, and its flaws are as instructive as its strengths.",
  [
    ["A prodigy turned mystic", "Pascal's wager comes from notes for an unfinished book by a mathematical genius who underwent a profound religious conversion.",
      "Blaise Pascal was born in France in 1623. As a teenager he wrote a notable work on conic sections, and at around nineteen he began building one of the first mechanical calculators to help his father with tax accounts. In 1654 he exchanged letters with Pierre de Fermat about dividing the stakes in an unfinished game of chance, helping lay the foundations of probability theory. That year, on 23 November, he had an intense religious experience, which he recorded on a paper he kept sewn into his coat.\n\nHe planned a great defence of Christianity, but died in 1662 aged 39, leaving fragments. Published in 1670 as the Pensées, they include the wager."],
    ["The infinite payoff", "Pascal argued that we cannot avoid the choice, and that an infinite reward outweighs any finite cost, however small the chance.",
      "Pascal did not claim to prove that God exists; he thought reason alone could not settle it. He asked instead: given that we cannot be certain, what is the rational way to live? We cannot sit on the fence, he said, because living as though God does not exist is itself a bet.\n\nHe then weighed the outcomes. Bet on God and God exists: eternal happiness, an infinite reward. Bet on God and God does not exist: you lose little, perhaps some worldly pleasures. Bet against God and God exists: you lose the infinite reward. Bet against and God does not exist: you gain only a finite amount. So any chance at all makes belief the rational bet. This is an early use of expected value: weighing each outcome by both its size and its probability."],
    ["I cannot make myself believe", "Pascal admitted that belief cannot be switched on at will, and suggested acting as believers do.",
      "An obvious objection is that you cannot choose to believe something just because it would be useful. Pascal anticipated this. He suggested that a person who wanted to believe but could not should begin by acting as believers do: attending Mass, taking holy water and following religious practice. Over time, he thought, habit would shape the heart and belief would follow.\n\nCritics find this troubling, since it seems to cultivate belief through habit rather than evidence. Defenders reply that Pascal was being psychologically realistic. Many of our deepest attitudes, from confidence to kindness, grow from repeated action rather than argument alone."],
    ["The many gods objection", "The strongest criticism is that the wager does not say which god to bet on, and that huge payoffs can justify almost anything.",
      "The most famous objection is that Pascal framed the choice as a simple pair: the Christian God or no god. But there are many religions with different demands, and some imagined gods might punish exactly the beliefs that others reward. Once all these possibilities are included, the wager no longer points clearly in one direction.\n\nOther critics question whether a god would reward belief adopted as a calculated bet, and whether infinite values can be handled sensibly in such calculations. A related puzzle, Pascal's mugging, has a stranger demanding money by promising an astronomically large reward. Tiny probabilities multiplied by huge payoffs can justify almost anything."],
    ["A lasting legacy", "Whatever its flaws, the wager pioneered a way of thinking about decisions under uncertainty.",
      "Few philosophers today think the wager settles whether one should believe in God. But its influence reaches far beyond religion. The philosopher of science Ian Hacking argued it was one of the earliest examples of decision theory, the study of how to choose rationally when outcomes are uncertain.\n\nIts structure of listing options, outcomes and probabilities, then weighing them, is now used in fields from insurance and medicine to public policy and climate planning. Debates about rare but catastrophic risks often echo Pascal's reasoning. When the stakes are high and the facts uncertain, how we frame a decision can matter as much as the evidence."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What question was Pascal's wager designed to answer?", ["Whether God's existence can be proven by logic","Which of the world's religions is the oldest","How to choose rationally when certainty is impossible","Whether science and faith are in conflict"], 2, "Pascal believed reason could not settle the question, so he asked how to choose wisely under uncertainty."],
    [1, "What is the key reason Pascal gives for betting on God?", ["Most people already believe in God","An infinite reward outweighs any finite cost","The Church commands everyone to believe","Reason has proven that God exists"], 1, "With an infinite payoff on one side and only finite pleasures on the other, belief seems the rational bet."],
    [2, "How did Pascal suggest someone who cannot believe should proceed?", ["Give up the attempt altogether","Read more about science and nature","Debate priests until he is convinced","Act as believers do and let belief follow"], 3, "He recommended religious practice, trusting that habit would gradually shape belief."],
    [3, "What is the 'many gods' objection?", ["It does not say which of many gods to bet on","There are too many churches to choose from","Gods are not interested in human belief","Pascal himself believed in several gods"], 0, "Once all possible religions and gods are included, the wager no longer points clearly to one choice."],
    [4, "Why does the wager matter beyond religion?", ["It proved that probability theory is wrong","It is an early example of decision theory","It showed that all gambling is rational","It settled the old debate about belief"], 1, "Pascal's method of weighing outcomes by size and likelihood is now used in insurance, medicine and public policy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A famous mathematician said believing in God is simply the smart bet. Is it?"});

WB.L("gettier-problem", "philosophy", "intermediate", "What is knowledge? The three page paper that broke a definition",
  "For over two thousand years, a neat definition of knowledge seemed good enough. Then, in 1963, a little known philosopher published three pages that sent the field scrambling.",
  [
    ["Knowledge versus luck", "Knowledge was long defined as justified true belief, because a lucky guess can be true without being knowledge.",
      "Suppose you believe it will rain tomorrow, and it does. Did you know? If you checked a reliable forecast, perhaps yes. If you had a hunch, you were only lucky. Epistemology, from the Greek word for knowledge, studies questions like this. Knowledge cannot be mere true belief, because a lucky guess can be true.\n\nIdeas reaching back to Plato's Theaetetus suggested an answer, which many 20th-century philosophers settled on: justified true belief. To know something, you must believe it, it must be true, and you must have good reasons or evidence. The forecast reader has justification; the hunch follower does not. For a long time this was treated as a neat standard definition."],
    ["Three pages in 1963", "Edmund Gettier showed in a three-page paper that a belief can be justified and true yet still not be knowledge.",
      "In 1963, Edmund Gettier, a young philosopher at Wayne State University in Detroit, published a paper in the journal Analysis titled Is Justified True Belief Knowledge? It ran to about three pages, and it is now one of the most widely cited articles in modern philosophy. Stories say he wrote it partly under pressure to publish, and he published little else.\n\nIn his first case, Smith has strong evidence that Jones will get a job, and has counted ten coins in Jones's pocket. He concludes that the successful applicant has ten coins. But Smith gets the job himself, and also has ten coins. His belief is true and justified, yet he did not know. He was right by coincidence."],
    ["Older cases of lucky truth", "Similar examples appeared long before Gettier, in India and in Bertrand Russell's writing.",
      "Gettier was not the first to notice the problem. In the 8th century, the Indian Buddhist philosopher Dharmottara described a traveller who sees a shimmering mirage, believes there is water ahead, and finds water hidden under a rock at that very spot.\n\nIn 1948, Bertrand Russell described someone who looks at a stopped clock that happens to show the right time. They form a justified true belief about the time, but do not really know it. Roderick Chisholm later offered a person who sees what looks like a sheep in a field. It is really a dog, but a genuine sheep happens to be out of sight elsewhere in the field. In each case, luck breaks the link between justification and truth."],
    ["The fourth condition", "Philosophers proposed many repairs, but each seemed to meet new counterexamples.",
      "Gettier's paper set off decades of attempts to fix the definition. One proposal added a fourth condition: the belief must not be reasoned from anything false. Smith's reasoning depended on the false belief that Jones would get the job. But critics found cases without obvious false steps, such as someone who looks at the one real barn in a district full of convincing fake barn fronts.\n\nThe causal theory said the fact must cause the belief in the right way. Reliabilism said knowledge comes from reliable belief-forming processes. Each approach handled some cases and stumbled on others. In 1994, Linda Zagzebski argued that as long as justification can fall short of guaranteeing truth, a clever enough counterexample can always be built."],
    ["Where things stand", "There is still no agreed definition of knowledge, and the lesson is that being right and knowing why are different.",
      "Today there is no universally accepted definition of knowledge. Timothy Williamson at Oxford argues that knowledge cannot be broken into simpler parts and should be treated as a basic concept in its own right. Others keep refining definitions. Experimental philosophers have tested Gettier cases on people from different cultures and found that many share the intuition that these lucky believers lack knowledge, though how widely is still debated.\n\nThe puzzle has practical echoes. In science, journalism and everyday life we often reach correct conclusions for the wrong reasons. Being right and knowing why are not the same thing."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is true belief alone not enough for knowledge?", ["Beliefs are never really true","A lucky guess can be true without being knowledge","Only scientists can have knowledge","Knowledge must be written down"], 1, "A hunch that happens to be correct is a true belief, but most people would not call it knowledge."],
    [1, "What did Gettier's 1963 paper show?", ["Knowledge is impossible for any human mind","Plato's account of knowledge was entirely right","Only scientific methods can produce knowledge","A justified true belief can still fail to be knowledge"], 3, "Gettier described cases that are true and justified but only by luck, so the classic definition was incomplete."],
    [2, "Why does the person in Russell's stopped-clock example not know the time?", ["They cannot read the clock face properly","The clock is running slightly too fast","The clock is stopped but happens to be right","They are dreaming about the time"], 2, "Their belief is true and justified, but only by coincidence, because the clock is not working."],
    [3, "What did Linda Zagzebski argue about attempts to fix the definition?", ["They have all finally succeeded in the end","Justification turns out to be unnecessary","Knowledge should be settled by majority vote","Clever new counterexamples can always be built"], 3, "Any definition that allows a gap between justification and truth leaves room for luck, and so for new Gettier cases."],
    [4, "What practical lesson does Gettier's puzzle offer?", ["Being right and knowing why are not the same thing","Evidence matters less than confidence","Experts are always correct","Guesses are as good as knowledge"], 0, "We often reach correct conclusions for the wrong reasons, so being right is not the same as knowing."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"You can be right, have good reasons, and still not know. How is that possible?"});

WB.L("machiavelli-prince", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Machiavelli: the man behind ‘The Prince’",
  "His name became a byword for cunning and ruthlessness. But the real Niccolò Machiavelli was a tortured civil servant, a lover of republics and perhaps the first modern political scientist.",
  [
    ["Servant, then prisoner", "Machiavelli spent fourteen years as a senior official of the Florentine Republic before being dismissed and tortured when the Medici returned.",
      "Niccolò Machiavelli was born in Florence in 1469. In 1498, after the Medici were driven out, he became a senior official of the new Florentine Republic, serving as secretary of its Second Chancery. For fourteen years he handled diplomacy and military affairs, visiting the courts of the French king, the pope and other rulers, and watching how power was gained and lost. He also organised a citizen militia, believing Florence should not rely on hired mercenaries.\n\nIn 1512, Spanish troops helped restore the Medici, and he lost his post. Early in 1513 his name appeared on a list of plotters and he was arrested. He endured the strappado, hoisting by the wrists tied behind the back, reportedly without confessing. A general amnesty freed him weeks later."],
    ["Evenings with the ancients", "Machiavelli wrote The Prince in 1513 in enforced retirement, hoping to win back favour.",
      "Machiavelli retired, impoverished, to his small farm outside Florence. In a famous letter to his friend Francesco Vettori in December 1513, he described his days. He snared birds, chatted with locals and played cards at the inn.\n\nBut in the evening he went to his study, took off his muddy clothes and put on formal robes. Then, as he put it, he entered the ancient courts of ancient men and asked them the reasons for their actions. From those conversations with history he composed a little work on principalities. That work was The Prince, written hoping to win back favour and employment. It was dedicated to a member of the Medici family, but it was not published until 1532, five years after his death in 1527."],
    ["Better feared than loved", "The Prince describes politics as it is, arguing that a ruler must sometimes act against conventional morality but must avoid being hated.",
      "The Prince shocked readers because it described politics as it is, not as it ought to be. Earlier advice books urged rulers to be generous, merciful and honest. Machiavelli argued that a ruler who always tried to be good would be ruined among so many who are not.\n\nIt is best to be both loved and feared, he wrote, but if one must choose, it is safer to be feared. Crucially, a ruler must avoid being hated, which usually means leaving subjects' property and families alone. A ruler should be both a fox, to recognise traps, and a lion, to frighten wolves.\n\nHis model was Cesare Borgia, who had his harsh deputy Remirro de Orco killed and displayed in Cesena. Machiavelli's point: cruelty well used is applied once, for security, and then stops."],
    ["Fortune and virtue", "Machiavelli believed skill and boldness could partly master the power of luck.",
      "Two words run through Machiavelli's writing: fortuna and virtù. Fortuna is luck or circumstance, the unpredictable force that can raise or ruin anyone. Virtù does not mean moral virtue. It means skill, energy and boldness, the capacity to seize opportunities and adapt.\n\nMachiavelli compared fortune to a violent river that floods the plains. When it rages, nothing can stop it, but in calm times people can build dykes and embankments to control it. He guessed that fortune governs about half of our actions and leaves the other half to us. A wise ruler prepares in good times and adapts quickly when circumstances change, rather than clinging to one way of acting."],
    ["The republican Machiavelli", "Machiavelli's longer work shows that he actually preferred republics to princes.",
      "The common image of Machiavelli as a cheerleader for tyranny is incomplete. His longer book, the Discourses on Livy, praises the Roman Republic and argues that free republics, where citizens share power and laws restrain ambition, are generally more stable and successful than principalities.\n\nEven the phrase 'the ends justify the means' does not appear in his writings, although he did argue that rulers are judged by results. In 1559 the Catholic Church placed his works on its Index of Prohibited Books, and Machiavellian came to mean scheming. Yet many modern scholars see him as a founder of political science, one of the first to study power by observing what actually happens."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Machiavelli's years as a Florentine official give him?", ["A fortune from the Medici","An insider's view of how power is gained and lost","A reputation as a general","A place in the royal court of France"], 1, "Fourteen years of diplomacy and military affairs let him watch closely how rulers won and lost power."],
    [1, "Why did Machiavelli write The Prince?", ["He wanted to overthrow the Medici family","He was commissioned by the pope himself","He hoped to win back favour and employment","He wanted to promote a free republic"], 2, "After losing his post and being tortured, he wrote a short book from history's lessons hoping to regain employment."],
    [2, "What did Machiavelli say about being feared or loved?", ["If a choice is needed, fear is safer, but avoid hatred","It is always better to be loved than feared","Fear and love are exactly the same thing","Rulers should aim to be hated by enemies"], 0, "Fear is more reliable than love, but hatred is dangerous, which a ruler avoids by respecting subjects' property and families."],
    [3, "In Machiavelli's writing, what does 'virtù' mean?", ["Moral goodness in public life","Religious faith and devotion","Wealth and status at court","Skill and boldness in seizing opportunities"], 3, "Virtù is the capacity to seize opportunities and adapt, which lets a person partly master fortune."],
    [4, "What does the Discourses on Livy reveal about Machiavelli?", ["He wanted Italy ruled by the pope","He admired free republics like Rome over principalities","He rejected all forms of government","He hoped to become a prince himself"], 1, "In the Discourses he praises the Roman Republic and argues free republics are generally more stable and successful."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"His name means cunning, yet he loved republics and was tortured for it. Who was he?"});

WB.L("nietzsche-misunderstood", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Nietzsche: the most misunderstood philosopher",
  "He is quoted on gym walls and was claimed by the Nazis, yet he despised antisemites and warned against blind nationalism. The real Friedrich Nietzsche is stranger and more interesting than his legend.",
  [
    ["A professor at twenty-four", "Nietzsche was a brilliant young scholar who broke with Wagner over nationalism and antisemitism.",
      "Friedrich Nietzsche was born in 1844 in Röcken, in Prussia, the son of a Lutheran pastor who died when he was four. In 1869, at just twenty-four, he became professor of classical philology at the University of Basel, before completing the usual doctoral requirements. His first book, The Birth of Tragedy, appeared in 1872. Severe headaches, eye problems and stomach troubles dogged him, and in 1879 he resigned and lived modestly in boarding houses across Europe, writing furiously.\n\nAs a young man he admired the composer Richard Wagner, but grew disillusioned with his German nationalism, his Christian themes in Parsifal and the surrounding antisemitism. Nietzsche repeatedly mocked nationalism and expressed contempt for antisemites."],
    ["The sister who rewrote him", "After Nietzsche's collapse his sister shaped his image, and later scholars worked to separate him from Nazi propaganda.",
      "In January 1889, in Turin, Nietzsche suffered a sudden mental collapse. A famous story says it followed his embracing a horse being whipped, though that may be embellished. He never recovered, and died in Weimar in 1900, cared for by his mother and then his sister Elisabeth.\n\nElisabeth, whose husband Bernhard Förster was an antisemitic agitator, controlled his manuscripts. She edited his letters selectively and assembled The Will to Power from his notes to suit her own views. She later welcomed the Nazis, who made selective use of phrases like the Übermensch while ignoring his contempt for antisemitism and nationalism. After the war, scholars such as Walter Kaufmann restored his reputation. Nietzsche's writing does contain harsh and elitist passages, but most scholars agree he was no proto-Nazi."],
    ["Eternal recurrence", "Nietzsche asked whether you could bear to live your exact life again, forever, as a test of how fully you affirm it.",
      "One of Nietzsche's most famous ideas appears in The Gay Science, published in 1882. He imagines a demon creeping into your loneliest loneliness and telling you that you must live your life again, exactly as it has been, every pain and joy in the same order, infinitely many times.\n\nWould you curse the demon, he asks, or call it divine? Nietzsche used this as a test of how fully you affirm your life. Scholars disagree about whether he meant it as a literal theory of the universe or as a thought experiment. Most read it as the latter: a way of asking whether you are living in a way you would choose again and again."],
    ["Love your fate", "Nietzsche's ideal, amor fati, was to love life's suffering rather than wish it away.",
      "Closely linked to eternal recurrence is amor fati, Latin for love of fate. Nietzsche wrote that his formula for greatness was wanting nothing to be different, not forwards, not backwards, not in all eternity, and not merely to bear what is necessary but to love it. This has much in common with Stoicism, though he criticised the Stoics on other points.\n\nIt also connects to his best-known line, from Twilight of the Idols in 1888: what does not kill me makes me stronger. Out of context it sounds like a motivational slogan. Nietzsche lived with chronic pain, and meant something more personal: suffering can be turned into a source of growth."],
    ["Beyond the slogans", "Nietzsche's deepest concern was how to create meaning after traditional values lost their grip.",
      "Nietzsche's statement that God is dead was not a triumphant boast but a warning. He feared that as religious belief faded in Europe, people would lose the foundation for their values and slide into nihilism, the sense that nothing matters.\n\nHis Übermensch was not a racial ideal but an image of a person who creates their own values and affirms life without outside guarantees. He also argued that there are no facts free of perspective, a view called perspectivism. His aphorisms, irony and exaggeration make him easy to quote and easy to misread. Reading him in full, rather than on a poster, reveals a restless, searching mind wrestling with problems that still feel modern."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Nietzsche break with Richard Wagner?", ["Wagner refused to perform his music","They quarrelled bitterly over money","Wagner publicly insulted his mother","He disliked Wagner's nationalism and antisemitism"], 3, "Nietzsche became disillusioned with Wagner's German nationalism, Christian themes and the antisemitism in his circle."],
    [1, "Why is the Nazi image of Nietzsche misleading?", ["He mocked antisemites, and his sister edited his notes","He openly supported the Nazis' political aims","He never wrote anything about power","His works were never published at all"], 0, "Elisabeth edited his work selectively and promoted a distorted image, while Nietzsche himself mocked nationalism and antisemites."],
    [2, "What does the idea of eternal recurrence ask?", ["Whether history repeats every century","Whether souls are reborn as animals","Whether you could affirm living your exact life again forever","Whether time travel is possible"], 2, "A demon announcing you must relive your life endlessly is a test of how fully you affirm the life you have."],
    [3, "What does 'amor fati' mean?", ["Fear of fate","Escape from fate","Change your fate","Love of fate"], 3, "Amor fati is Nietzsche's ideal of embracing everything that happens, even suffering, rather than wishing it otherwise."],
    [4, "What was Nietzsche's 'God is dead' statement meant as?", ["A triumphant boast about the rise of atheism","A warning that losing faith could lead to nihilism","A call for governments to ban religion","A prediction that science would end suffering"], 1, "Nietzsche feared that as faith faded, people would lose meaning, which is why he asked how to create it ourselves."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Quoted on gym walls, claimed by Nazis, yet he despised their ideas. What did he mean?"});

WB.L("sorites-paradox", "philosophy", "advanced", "The paradox of the heap: where do boundaries go?",
  "One grain of sand is not a heap. Adding a single grain never turns a non-heap into a heap. So, it seems, there are no heaps at all. This ancient puzzle about vagueness still divides logicians today.",
  [
    ["The puzzle of the heap", "Tiny, harmless steps can seem to prove an absurd conclusion about a vague word like heap.",
      "One grain of sand is not a heap. Adding a single grain to something that is not a heap never makes it a heap. So two grains is not a heap, nor three, nor a million. Yet a million grains clearly is a heap. Each premise looks true and each step looks valid, but the conclusion is absurd.\n\nThis is the sorites paradox, named from soros, the Greek word for heap. It is credited to Eubulides of Miletus, a 4th century BC philosopher also linked to the liar paradox. A matching version, the bald man, runs the other way: removing one hair never turns a man who is not bald into a bald one."],
    ["Vagueness everywhere", "The sorites paradox arises with almost any word that has borderline cases, so it challenges how logic handles ordinary language.",
      "The sorites is not really about sand. It arises with any vague term: a word with borderline cases where it is unclear whether it applies. Tall, old, rich, red, child and crowd are all vague.\n\nSomeone 190 centimetres tall is clearly tall and someone 150 centimetres is not, but what about 175? There seems to be no single millimetre at which a person becomes tall. Yet if no small change can make a difference, the same reasoning concludes that nobody is tall. Most everyday words are vague, so this is no mere curiosity. It asks whether ordinary logic, built for sharp categories, can handle ordinary language."],
    ["A hidden sharp line", "Epistemicism holds that vague words have sharp boundaries that we can never know.",
      "The Stoic Chrysippus, a great logician of the ancient world, is reported to have advised a wise person to fall silent somewhere before the doubtful cases and decline to answer. Critics mocked this. But some modern scholars think he may have meant that a boundary exists and cannot be known.\n\nThat is close to epistemicism, defended by the British philosopher Timothy Williamson in his 1994 book Vagueness. It says there is a precise number of grains at which a pile becomes a heap. We cannot know it, because meaning is fixed by patterns of use too complex to survey. This keeps classical logic intact, but many find it unbelievable that one grain could make the difference."],
    ["Changing the logic", "Other theories change how truth works, but they all struggle to say where borderline cases begin.",
      "Supervaluationism, developed in a 1975 paper by the philosopher Kit Fine, imagines every acceptable way of making a vague word precise. A statement is true if it is true on every sharpening, false if false on every one, and neither otherwise.\n\nAnother approach uses degrees of truth, drawing on fuzzy logic introduced by the engineer Lotfi Zadeh in 1965. On this view, 'this pile is a heap' might be true to degree 0.7. Critics note that both theories face higher order vagueness: where exactly do the borderline cases begin?\n\nThe American philosopher Peter Unger went further and accepted the paradox's conclusion, arguing that ordinary vague objects such as heaps do not strictly exist."],
    ["Bright lines in life", "When boundaries are blurred, societies draw arbitrary but useful lines, and clear thinking means knowing when to rely on them.",
      "In practice we handle vagueness with bright lines. Childhood fades into adulthood gradually, yet laws set fixed ages for voting, driving and drinking. Nobody changes overnight on their eighteenth birthday, but a clear rule is fairer and easier to apply than case by case judgement. Speed limits, blood alcohol thresholds and pass marks work the same way.\n\nThe sorites also underlies slippery slope arguments, which claim that because no single step makes a difference, we should not take the first step. Recognising the structure helps you judge when such arguments hold. Medical definitions, legal standards like reasonable care and debates about when life begins all lack sharp edges. Sometimes clear thinking means living with blurred boundaries."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes the heap argument a paradox?", ["The premises are obviously false from the start","It relies on a mistake in basic arithmetic","Each small step seems valid, yet the conclusion is plainly absurd","Its conclusion is true but nobody can prove it"], 2, "Plausible premises and apparently valid steps lead to a conclusion we know to be wrong, which is what makes it a paradox."],
    [1, "Why does the paradox matter beyond sand and hair?", ["It arises with any vague word that has borderline cases, such as tall or old","It only affects words used in mathematics","It applies only to words naming physical objects","It shows that numbers cannot be counted"], 0, "Most everyday words are vague, so the problem challenges how logic copes with ordinary language."],
    [2, "What does epistemicism claim about vague words?", ["They have no boundaries at all","Their boundaries shift with each speaker's mood","They are meaningless and should be avoided","They have sharp boundaries that we cannot know"], 3, "Epistemicism keeps classical logic by saying the lines are real but hidden from us."],
    [3, "On the supervaluationist view, when is a vague statement true?", ["When it is true on most people's personal definitions of the word","When it is true on every acceptable way of making the word precise","When it is true to a degree of at least one half or higher","When it can be verified by measuring the object directly"], 1, "A statement true on some sharpenings and false on others is treated as neither true nor false."],
    [4, "Why do laws set fixed ages for voting or driving, even though people mature gradually?", ["Everyone truly changes overnight at that age","Vague concepts cannot be regulated by law","A clear rule is fairer and easier to apply than case by case judgement","Judges are not allowed to consider individual cases"], 2, "Bright lines are arbitrary but practical ways of handling gradual change."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"One grain is not a heap. Neither are two. So where do heaps come from?"});

